--TEST--
`Core\Http\Part::file` sends a file as the whole body or as one multipart field, named by its path where the call names nothing, and read again at every call — `rule:http-server/an-outbound-request-carries-one-body`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A part is a *description of what to send*, not a copy of it: nothing between
// the disk and the socket holds the file, which is the whole reason `body`
// admits a `Core\Http\Part` beside its two octet arms. What a case can see of
// that is what arrives — so the claim here is the framing, byte for byte, with
// the per-call boundary normalised away.
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://api.example.com/*", 200, {body: "ok"});
Core\IO::write("upload.txt", "an upload\n");

// As the whole body: the octets are the file's and the type is the call's own
// `contentType`, which is the key that types octets and means nothing without
// them.
var $whole = Core\Http\Client::put("https://api.example.com/objects/1", {
    body: Core\Http\Part::file("upload.txt"),
    contentType: "text/plain",
});

// As one field of a multipart body, beside a field that is just a value. The
// part names no `filename`, so it is told the path's last component; it names
// no type, so the segment is `application/octet-stream`.
var $field = Core\Http\Client::post("https://api.example.com/uploads", {multipart: [
    "note" => "from the case",
    "attachment" => Core\Http\Part::file("upload.txt"),
]});

// The same part value written to two calls, with the file rewritten between
// them: a part holds a path and not the octets, so the second call sends what
// is on disk when it is made rather than what was there when the part was
// built.
var $part = Core\Http\Part::file("upload.txt", {filename: "renamed.txt", contentType: "text/plain"});
var $first = Core\Http\Client::put("https://api.example.com/objects/2", {body: $part});
Core\IO::write("upload.txt", "the second upload, longer\n");
var $second = Core\Http\Client::put("https://api.example.com/objects/2", {body: $part});

// The other arm of the same class, and the same position: a `bytes` part is a
// part whose octets the program is already holding, so it carries a type the
// way a file part does and reads nothing.
var $held = Core\Http\Client::put("https://api.example.com/objects/3", {
    body: Core\Http\Part::bytes("held octets", "held.txt", {contentType: "text/plain"}),
});

foreach (Core\Test::sentHttp() as Core\Test\SentRequest $call) {
    string $type = $call->header("content-type") ?? "-";
    string $boundary = Core\Str::after($type, "boundary=") ?? "";
    string $framed = $call->body() as string;
    if ($boundary != "") {
        $type = Core\Str::before($type, ";") ?? $type;
        // The dashes go with it: a `--` at the start of a line is how this
        // file's own sections are spelled, and a frozen expectation cannot
        // carry one. The line endings are the wire's and go the same way, for
        // the same reason.
        $framed = Core\Str::replaceAll($framed, ["--" . $boundary => "=SEGMENT=", "\r\n" => "\n"]);
    }
    echo $call->url(), " ", $type, " ", Core\Bytes::length($call->body()), "\n";
    echo $framed, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The grant the part is built under. `Core\Http\Part::file` asks for it where
# the path is written, and the framing asks again where the file is opened, so
# an ungranted path never reaches a socket.
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
https://api.example.com/objects/1 text/plain 10
an upload

https://api.example.com/uploads multipart/form-data 351
=SEGMENT=
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="note"

from the case
=SEGMENT=
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="attachment"; filename="upload.txt"
Content-Type: application/octet-stream

an upload

=SEGMENT=--

https://api.example.com/objects/2 text/plain 10
an upload

https://api.example.com/objects/2 text/plain 26
the second upload, longer

https://api.example.com/objects/3 text/plain 11
held octets

